{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791146886,"nonce":"7EwZU8bDhM8bkKFw4YNiAofpaIo=","title":"3D textures: BC compression, and an optional size cap","message":"The DamagedHelmet's five 2048² textures are 112MB on the GPU as RGBA8 with full mip chains. That is most of the studio demo's footprint: 288MB AOT, about 370MB hybrid.\n\n**What Blinc does** (from a study of crates/blinc_gpu and packages/blinc_game_kit):\n- Formats:\n  - base colour BC1/BC3 sRGB;\n  - normal BC5 (RG, z rebuilt in the shader);\n  - occlusion BC4;\n  - metallic-roughness and emissive stay RGBA8.\n- Encoding is done on the CPU with the `tbc` crate (blinc_gpu/src/bc_encode.rs, blinc_game_kit/src/gltf/bc_encode.rs), about 50–150ms per 2K texture.\n- The device feature TEXTURE_COMPRESSION_BC is requested only when the adapter has it; without it, RGBA8 is used.\n- The glTF loader has an opt-in `max_texture_size` that downscales.\n- Blinc skips mips on mesh textures. We keep them, for quality at a distance.\n\n**Plan**\n- Bind Blinc's BC encoder in blinc_abi. It is Blinc code, so it belongs there.\n- Encode each mip level after it is resampled, and upload as BC when the adapter has the feature.\n- Rebuild the normal's z in MeshShader for BC5.\n- Add `Gltf.load(path, {maxTextureSize})`.\n\n**Expected:** about 40–55MB for the helmet's textures instead of 112MB.","files":null}]}